AB  - Dance Movement Therapy (DMT) has emerged as a promising intervention within psychiatric and neurological contexts, offering embodied, non-verbal avenues for expression, integration, and connection. This review explores DMT applications for individuals diagnosed with psychosis, depression, Parkinson's Disease, and dementia—four populations characterized by disruptions in movement, affect, and cognition. DMT has shown potential to impact quality of life indicators, body schema reorganization, affect regulation, and interpersonal functioning. Drawing from empirical literature, neurocognitive frameworks, and clinical practice, this chapter synthesizes data from randomized controlled trials, meta-analyses, and qualitative studies to illuminate DMT's mechanisms and effects. It also discusses the importance of including caregivers, leveraging technology for accessibility, and framing DMT within recovery-oriented and interdisciplinary care. Implications for future research, education, and policy are addressed.
