AB  - This longitudinal study investigated the long-term psychophysiological restorative effects of combining music and soundscapes in a virtual environment. It explores the potential of this approach as a sustainable non-pharmacological strategy for stress reduction and mental well-being. Fifty-nine young adults with mild stress symptoms participated three times per week for 6 weeks in a cave automated virtual environment. Psychophysiological responses were assessed at weeks 1, 6, and 10. Results indicated a significant reduction in perceived stress, particularly after week 6, sustained through week 10. Heart rate variability indicators, including total power and standard deviation of normal-to-normal intervals, showed notable improvements. Electroencephalography analysis demonstrated increased functional connectivity in the frontal cortex, with marked increases in theta and alpha band activity, supporting enhanced concentration and relaxation. Global efficiency, particularly in the alpha band, improved. This suggests that music–soundscape combinations can be effective non-pharmacological interventions, contributing to long-term stress management and mental well-being with sustained psychophysiological benefits.
