AB  - The issues of stress and mental fatigue are usually common, which makes a necessity to find objective and real-time ways of tracking the state of relaxation. Well-established neural indicators of the relaxed and calm mental states include alpha brainwaves (8–12 Hz). Despite the traditional use of Vedic chanting in stress reduction, its scientific validation has been low since it has depended on subjective evaluation, indirect acoustic evaluation and a standard EEG methodology that is not adaptive signal processing. To overcome these drawbacks, this research suggests a combined electrochemical-spectral model to quantitatively measure the alpha brainwave activation to Vedic chanting. EEG signals were recorded using standard international 10–20 scalp electrode placements during baseline resting conditions and chanting sessions involving Sama, Rig, and Yajur Veda chants. The acquired EEG data were preprocessed using Kalman filtering for real-time noise suppression and Z-score normalization to ensure inter-channel comparability. Alpha-band activity was isolated through frequency band analysis and examined using Fast Fourier Transform (FFT), Power Spectral Density (PSD), and the Hilbert–Huang Transform (HHT) to capture both spectral characteristics and non-stationary instantaneous dynamics. Statistical validation was performed using Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) in SPSS to compare alpha-band energy across baseline and chanting conditions. The results demonstrate significant alpha brainwave modulation, with chanting producing statistically meaningful differences compared to baseline conditions (p < 0.0005). It allows adjusting and timely, statistically verified evaluation of brainwave reactions to relaxation, which confirms its relevance in objective stress readings and neurofeedback-based systems of mental health.
