AB  - This article is about assessing and managing depression in children and adolescents. Effective management involves accurate diagnosis, careful monitoring of symptoms, and a comprehensive treatment plan that may include lifestyle changes, psychotherapy, and medication. Specific evidence-based practices, such as cognitive behavioral therapy, interpersonal therapy, and antidepressant medication have proven effective in reducing depression symptoms, and are generally considered first-line treatments. A thorough management strategy should take a holistic approach, also addressing physical health, sleep, and diet, while involving family, school, social, and spiritual support to improve the chances of a full recovery. In severe or hard-to-treat cases, options like electroconvulsive therapy and inpatient hospitalization may be necessary. Emerging treatments such as transcranial magnetic therapy, triple chronotherapy, and ketamine show potential, but more research is needed. This article emphasizes a “stepped care” approach to treatment, which takes into account the severity of the depression symptoms to deliver specific treatments that strike the best balance between efficacy, side effects, and potential life disruption from engaging in the treatments.
