AB  - Research Objectives
To assess compliance with repeated heart rate variability (HRV) and ecological momentary assessments (EMA) via mobile questionnaires and a smartphone application over a two-week period.
Design
Repeated measures design.
Setting
Community.
Participants
Adults with a self-reported history of TBI (n=52; n=12 mild TBI [mTBI] without loss of consciousness [LOC], n=22 mTBI with LOC, n=18 moderate-to-severe TBI).
Interventions
N/A.
Main Outcome Measures
Measures of compliance (total number of completed assessments divided by number of scheduled assessments) for HRV and EMA. Participants instructed to complete HRV measures twice daily for 14 days. EMA conducted using the Behavioral Assessment Screening Tool Short Form (BAST-SF). Participants randomized to one of three study arms (Arm 1: BAST-SF sent every day at a scheduled time; Arm 2: BAST-SF sent every day at a random time; Arm 3: BAST-SF sent every other day at a scheduled time).
Results
Compliance with EMA calculated among all participants and within each study arm (n=17 in Arm 1, n=18 in Arm 2, n=17 in Arm 3). Participants completed 496 of 609 scheduled assessments (81.4%). For “completers” (completed >2 daily measures; n=44), participants completed 494 of 511 scheduled assessments (96.7%). Among all participants and completers, those in Arm 1 demonstrated the least compliance (79.4% and 89.0%), while those in Arm 3 demonstrated the most compliance (86.6% and 98.1%). Compliance with HRV measures calculated among all participants and by injury severity. Participants completed 1055 of 1456 scheduled HRV measures (72.5%). For “completers” (completed ≥ 1 HRV measure; n=44), participants completed 1055 of 1232 scheduled HRV measures (85.6%). Among all participants and completers, those with a mTBI with LOC demonstrated the least compliance (55.8% and 81.9%). Those with a moderate-to-severe TBI (among all participants) and those with a mTBI without LOC (among completers) demonstrated the most compliance (84.9% and 91.9%).
Conclusions
HRV and EMA via smartphone demonstrates initial feasibility and compliance among adults with TBI. This presents an opportunity for long-term monitoring and pairing of neurobehavioral and physiological symptom post-TBI, potentially leading to more personalized clinical interventions.
Author(s) Disclosures
NIH/NICHD R03HD094445.
