AB  - Complementary and integrative health approaches include a broad range of physical, psychological, and nutritional interventions that are not typically part of conventional medical care or may have origins outside of conventional Western practice. They are widely used by women in the United States, and there is growing interest in their potential value for symptom management. The quality of the evidence on the efficacy and safety of complementary and integrative therapies is evolving, and there are areas of promise for treating difficult-to-manage conditions such as chronic pain; conditions related to mental health or women’s reproductive health; and neurological, cardiometabolic, and autoimmune disorders. The National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health supports the full continuum of the research pipeline on complementary and integrative therapies, from basic and mechanistic research through efficacy trials and dissemination and implementation research, including studies in real-world settings and understudied populations.
