AB  - The well-being of healthcare professionals (HCPs) is increasingly recognized as a critical factor for both individual health and the functioning of healthcare systems. This chapter explores well-being as a multidimensional construct, encompassing physical, psychological, and social domains. Empirical findings highlight the adverse effects of shift work, chronic stress, and emotional burden on appetite, sleep, cognitive performance, and emotional stability among HCPs. Key risk phenomena such as burnout and moral injury are prevalent and linked to reduced job satisfaction, impaired health, and increased intent to leave the profession. In addition to subjective assessments, physiological indicators including heart rate variability, cortisol levels, and neuroimaging data provide objective insights into stress-related strain. Psychosocial resources such as self-esteem, social support, and meaningfulness of work serve as protective factors. The chapter also reviews evidence-based interventions, including mindfulness, physical activity, and creative therapies, which have shown positive effects on well-being.
